russia's invasion of ukraine is said to  dominate a meeting of g20 foreign  ministers the group of rich and  developing nations have begun their  talks on the indonesian island of bali  the countries involved are ones likely  to feel the effects of a food and energy  crisis that the war is setting off  russian foreign minister sergey lavrov  opened his visit by holding talks with  his chinese counterpart wang yi wang is  also expected to meet face to face with  u.s secretary of state anthony blinken  u.s china tensions remain high over  taiwan and trade disputes china has also  refused to condemn russia's invasion of  ukraine while the us is sending military  gear and other assistance to bolster  kiev's war effort  amid the tensions indonesian president  joko widodo has been trying to mediate  last week he visited both russia and  ukraine in what he described as a peace  mission here's more on indonesia's  unique stance on the war in ukraine  joko widodo has sought to maintain good  relations with both russia and ukraine  indonesia is what vladimir putin calls a  friendly nation for moscow  unlike any of the western powers  widodo has refused to give in to western  pressure to exclude vladimir putin from  the summit of g20 leaders in november  his visit to moscow and kiev last week  reflected a delicate balancing act  what i said in kiev i repeat here  though the current situation is still  hard we must keep opening more room for  dialogue i have conveyed a message from  president zielinski to president putin  and i express my readiness to become a  communication bridge between the two  leaders  the content of that message from  zalenski was not made public  widodo was the first asian leader to  visit kiev since the war began  he invited president zielenski to attend  the g20 leaders summit in november even  though ukraine is not part of the group  widodo has expressed sympathy with  ukrainians over the war and said the  country's territorial integrity must be  respected  prior to the meeting he toured the kiev  suburb of epeen to witness the  devastation first hand  indonesia is sending humanitarian aid to  ukraine but has declined to provide  weapons for use against russian forces  widodo came fresh from an international  summit in germany where he was able to  compare notes with western leaders and  meet with those who have previously  sought to persuade vladimir putin to end  the war indonesia has had success in the  past in mediating in regional conflicts  in asia but widodo says he has no  illusions about the complexity of the  russia ukraine war  and in a reminder of those complexities  zelensky hinted he would only attend the  g20 summit if putin is not there but the  kremlin says putin has already confirmed  his attendance  let's go to jakarta now and speak with  lena alexandra head of the international  relations department at the center for  strategic and international studies  welcome to dw news asia you were quoted  this week in malaysia's the star  newspaper as saying that joko widodo's  trip to ukraine was not a peace effort  so why did he go  well basically i think we need to  clarify first uh i think from the press  statements that the president made after  the meeting with the ukraine ukrainian  and president and also  the russian president i think it's quite  clear that there are several uh goals or  objectives of him going  to visit the europe  basically the first is to  reiterate or to emphasize the  invitations to most countries to attend  the g20 summit in november in bali  that's the first message  second  is of course to express the the the  empathy and also the concern regarding  the conflict uh the ongoing conflict  between russia and ukraine  and also  in that context he also mentions that he  is actually willing to be the the the  messenger  of a peace message um from the ukrainian  president to the russian president and  the third point i think this is very  very clear that  indonesia the indonesian president  expresses  the hope that  both countries can also can can return  to the global supply chain  where  the commodities from both countries  can uh  uh can can uh can go back to the global  super chain so it can actually avoid the  the crisis or the food crisis the energy  crisis uh that is actually um facing or  haunting  uh many states um  including in southeast asia okay you've  you've outlined possibly uh some of the  reasons i'm going to ask you on my next  question but if you could sort of go  into more detail as to why the war  matters to indonesia and more broadly to  southeast asia  well i think to some extent we need to  admit we need to recognize that the  in terms of geographical sense  that the conflict itself is quite remote  from the from the region but of course i  think it is very clear for everyone that  the implications of the war especially  in terms of the direct disruption of  global supply chain this is something  that really implicates the region very  much especially with the with the supply  of barley and also potassium to make  fertilizers things like that that really  implicates the region that is still  suffering from the impact of the coffee  19 panamix  talk to us about why is indonesia  choosing to stay neutral in this  conflict  well i think for  different reasons what i can say  i think it is very clear we recognize  ourselves that we don't really  we don't really have the capacity to be  the so-called peace mediator for example  but we are the country that actually um  yeah are implicated the most  uh from the impacts of of the war itself  as i mentioned earlier it is  for example indonesia is one of the  biggest importers of the  bali from ukraine for example so this is  something that we need to um  to deal with and that's why i think the  president needs to emphasize on this  that's one point  second point is definitely because  indonesia holds the position as the  president of the g20  and there is also an interest  to make sure  that we can secure  the g20 agenda later on in the summit  because of course we all know that there  has been issue that some countries might  uh not want to appear in the summit  later on so as a president i think  indonesia needs to make sure about this  do you think indonesia sees this too  possibly as an opportunity  to improve its international image  well of course i think that's quite  natural of course  but at this moment i think the focus is  really first  to uh  break to ensure that these countries  that are conflict conflicting each other  that they return to glo to the global  supply chain in order to mitigate the  the current food crisis that we are  suffering uh including in the southeast  asia region  uh and of course the second thing is to  make sure  that the the g20 is successful under  indonesia's chairmanship  okay that was lena alexandra the head of  the international relations department  at the center for strategic and  international studies in jakarta thank  you very much for your analysis  thank you
 

